Understanding the Schema

The database contains ~80 tables organized into 11 logical domains:

Core: user accounts, persons, organizations, roles

League Structure: seasons, divisions, teams

People: players, rosters, lineups, equipment

Games & Events: games, game_events (live scoring), venues, standings

Statistics: player/team/goalie stats, achievements, milestones

Admin: waivers, transfers, suspensions, draft, approvals

Playoffs: brackets, seedings

Community: events, RSVP, messaging

Operations: game attendance, periods, penalty box, practice sessions

Settings: integrations (Shopify, Sheets, Apps Script), definitions

Helpers/Cache: lookup and denormalized tables for fast Sheets queries

Key insight: Helper tables (player_lookup, division_team_lookup, team_season_rosters, season_current, etc.) are pre-computed snapshots designed to speed up Sheets filtering and dropdown validation. Keep these updated when core data changes.

Google Sheets Schema Conversion

Create one sheet per table. Optimization for Sheets:

Primary keys: Keep id as the first column (auto-increment via GAS helper formulas if needed).

Foreign keys: Store as numeric IDs, but add adjacent lookup columns (e.g., team_id + team_name from division_team_lookup for readability).

Enums: Create a hidden reference sheet (_Enums) with one row per enum type; use Data Validation (dropdown) on enum columns.

Timestamps: Store as ISO 8601 strings (e.g., 2024-01-15T14:30:00Z); GAS can format on display.

Denormalized columns: Helper tables have duplicated data (e.g., player_lookup.first_name + last_name copied from persons). Update these via GAS triggers when the source changes.

Index & unique constraints: Add sheet-level notes or conditional formatting to highlight duplicates; GAS validation can enforce uniqueness on form submission.

Sheet Naming Convention:

Use snake_case matching table names: league_structure, game_events, player_lookup, etc.

Critical sheets for Phase 1 (build first):

organizations, persons, user_accounts, roles

seasons, divisions, teams

